# portico — the hexastyle Ionic portico: six columns, two antae, and the coffered soffit over them
#
# The face of the building. Six columns of the order in a row on the podium at y = 1.80, their
# axes on the contract's six numbers, with the antae returning to the south wall at z = -7.50 and
# the coffered ceiling closing the whole thing over at y = 9.90.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD — do not "tidy" any of it away.
#
# THE DEEPEST EXTERIOR SHADOW IN THE BUILDING, AND THE HARDEST AMBIENT-OCCLUSION GRADIENT.
#
# A portico is a room with one wall missing. This one is 15.30 m across, 3.65 m deep from the
# column axis to the wall, and 8.10 m from the floor to the ceiling, and it is roofed. Direct sun
# reaches the front third of the floor and NOTHING ELSE: every other surface in here — the north
# face of six shafts, both antae, the whole coffered ceiling — is lit only by light that has
# bounced at least once off the podium. That is the one place in this building where the path
# tracer has nowhere to hide, and it is why this part exists.
#
# Three things are stacked inside that shadow on purpose, hardest last:
#
#   1. SIX FLUTED SHAFTS IN A ROW. Each is the order's `ionic_column` — 24 flutes, and the flat
#      fillet between two of them is 0.030 m, ONE VOXEL at the contract's metre 32. Six of them
#      at 2.70 m centres means the gradient across a shaft (bright fillet, dark flute, bright
#      fillet) repeats 144 times across the elevation, and it does it in a light that has no
#      direct term at all. If the sampler, the mesher or the denoiser is going to turn a one-voxel
#      feature into grey mud, it turns THIS into grey mud, and it does it six times over.
#
#   2. TWENTY COFFERS OVERHEAD. `coffer_unit` from the order is a 0.90 m square hollow 0.225 m
#      deep, stepping back in three moulded courses whose steps are 0.0225 m — UNDER a voxel. A
#      coffer is the textbook soft occlusion gradient: a small square hollow in a ceiling that is
#      itself in shadow, so the only light in it is second-bounce light that got into a box with
#      one open face. Twenty of them, side by side, is twenty chances to see banding, splotching
#      or a flat grey pan where there should be a gradient from the lip to the corner.
#
#   3. A GILT ROSETTE AT THE BACK OF EVERY ONE. 0.18 m square, hanging 0.075 m below the pan, so
#      it sits 0.195 m up inside a hollow that is itself in permanent shade. `gilt` is rough=64
#      metal=225 — a sharp specular metal — and a sharp specular metal lit ONLY by third-bounce
#      light is the single nastiest thing in this scene for an importance sampler. It is either a
#      warm point of light at the back of each coffer or it is black, and which of those it comes
#      out as is the whole verdict on the indirect path.
#
# There is also a rough/polished pair here to be judged side by side in the same light and nowhere
# else: limestone (rough=205) ceiling against marble (rough=140) columns, 0.05 m apart at the
# abacus, both of them in bounce light only.
#
# NO WEATHERING IN THIS FILE, DELIBERATELY. Every weathering coat displaces the surface, and the
# smallest amount worth writing is several times the 0.030 m fillet between two flutes and an
# order of magnitude over the 0.0225 m step in a coffer moulding. Weathering the portico would
# sand off precisely the features this part is here to test. The manifest's 12 mm of grain and the
# `variation` line give it the no-two-voxels-alike surface without touching the thin matter.
#
# THE DRUM JOINTS ARE NOT AN EXCEPTION TO THAT AND IT IS WORTH BEING PRECISE ABOUT WHY. A coat
# displaces the WHOLE surface by a fraction of a voxel — every fillet at once, everywhere, which is
# the thing that turns a one-voxel feature into mud. A bed joint is a groove 0.045 m tall cut at
# seven named heights and nowhere else: it is a whole voxel and a half deep, so it is matter rather
# than dither, and between two of them 0.855 m of fillet and flute is untouched. The rule this file
# is defending is "nothing may move the thin matter", not "nothing may be cut".
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE MODULE. D = 0.90, M = 0.45. Every number below is one of those, a multiple, or a simple
# fraction. Three are not, and all three are named where they occur:
#
#   z = -11.15   the column axis, the contract's own — 1.35 (3M) behind the portico line at -12.50
#   0.50         the band where the ceiling meets the wall: what the contract's 5.00 m portico
#                depth leaves over once the coffers are laid out from the column axis
#   0.05         the overlap the brief asks for wherever this part stands on somebody else's, and
#                0.02 the same thing for a paint test. Both are the building's constants, not
#                dimensions of anything — nothing is measured from either.

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE COLUMNS — six, hexastyle, on the podium
# ================================================================================================
#
# Axes at x = +-1.35, +-4.05, +-6.75 (3M, 9M, 15M): eustyle, 3 D between axes, the contract's own
# six numbers and the spine of the whole south elevation. All six stand on z = -11.15, which is
# 1.35 (3M) behind the portico's front line at -12.50.
#
# Built once as a unit at the origin and carried to three places on +x, then folded. Three
# translates and a mirror rather than a repeat, because a rigid motion is exact and a fold is
# exact, and both let the sampler settle whole boxes; see the note in _order.clip about what a
# polar repeat costs this building.

# The shoe. The column's own plinth starts at 1.80 and so does the podium, and a butt joint on a
# surface the manifest later displaces is a hairline crack waiting to happen. This is the plinth's
# own footprint (1.35 square, 3M) sunk 0.05 into the podium and reaching 0.05 up inside the
# plinth, so it is invisible from every angle and the join is certain.
let portico_col_shoe = box -0.675 -0.05 -0.675   0.675 0.05 0.675  round=0.015

# The bed, and the same argument at the top of the column: the abacus finishes at 9.90 and the
# soffit begins at 9.90. This block straddles that line by 0.05 either side, M/3 square, small
# enough to be inside the abacus below the line and inside the ceiling above it wherever it is
# used. It never shows, because at both ends it is buried in stone that is already there.
#
# It is drawn only on +z — the north half — because the soffit does not reach south of the column
# axis, and this is also the anta's bed, which after its half turn is drawn only on -z. One shape,
# used twice, on the only side both of them have in common.
let portico_bed = box -0.15 8.05 0   0.15 8.15 0.15

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FLUTES, WHICH WERE NOT IN THE BUILDING. Read this before touching anything below it.
#
# `_order.clip` cuts the twenty-four flutes by shrinking the shaft and putting the surface back on
# twenty-four flat fins:
#
#     let shaft_floor = offset { shaft_solid } by=-0.0375
#     let order_shaft = intersection { shaft_solid union { shaft_floor shaft_fins } }
#
# `offset` is `d + by` — the node's own comment in field.hpp says so — so `by=-0.0375` GROWS the
# shaft by 0.0375 instead of shrinking it. The union of a grown shaft with anything contains the
# shaft, so the intersection is the shaft, and `order_shaft` is a plain turned column. Sliced at
# mid-height, at metre 32, it is a circle: thirty voxels across, not one notch in it. See
# requests/portico-flute-probe.clip, which is four lines and answers it in two seconds.
#
# Nothing reported it. The clip parses, the part builds, the extent is right to the millimetre, the
# component count is 1 and every paint rule fires. A cut that removes nothing is the silent half of
# this failure mode and this is the largest instance of it in the building: the portico's first load
# note is SIX FLUTED SHAFTS IN A ROW and the one-voxel fillet between two flutes repeated 144 times
# across the elevation, and none of it existed. The eighteen pilasters and these two antae were
# never affected — `ionic_pilaster` cuts its flutes with plain boxes and is correct — so the fault
# had a fluted anta standing against an unfluted column at the end of the same colonnade.
#
# THE FIX HERE IS LOCAL AND IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT A NEW COLUMN. `_order.clip` is not this
# fragment's to edit and the order is right about what happens to a column drawn twice. So the flute
# floor is rebuilt from the order's OWN `shaft_band` and `shaft_arc`, and what that leaves standing
# over the order's own `shaft_fins` is subtracted from `ionic_column` — the flutes it should have cut
# and did not. No dimension is restated here; if `_order.clip` is fixed, this subtracts a set that is
# already absent, which costs a max() and changes nothing. Written up in requests/portico.md.
#
# AND THE FLOOR IS THE PROFILE SHRUNK, NOT THE SOLID OFFSET, WHICH IS THE SECOND HALF OF THE TRAP.
# `offset { shaft_solid } by=0.0375` — the corrected sign — still does not give a flute floor. A
# `revolve` measures distance in the profile's own plane, and this profile's inner edge is the axis
# itself, so on the axis the field reads 0 rather than -0.47; shrink the SOLID and the first 0.0375 m
# of the axis is outside the result, so the shaft comes out with a 0.075 m bore drilled down the
# middle of it. It is not visible from outside and it was in the first version of this line: a slice
# at mid-height showed a two-voxel hole dead on centre, which is the only place it could be seen.
#
# Shrinking the ARC instead moves only the curve the surface is struck on. The band still reaches
# r = 0, the axis stays solid, and the floor stays parallel to the entasis all the way up, which is
# the whole reason the order used an offset rather than a straight cutter in the first place.
let portico_shaft_floor = revolve {
    intersection { shaft_band  offset { shaft_arc } by=0.0375 }
} axis=y
let portico_flute_gap = difference { shaft_solid  portico_shaft_floor  shaft_fins }
let portico_flutes    = translate { portico_flute_gap } 0 0.45 0
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# THE DRUM JOINTS — seven bed joints up each shaft, one every diameter.
#
# A shaft 7.20 m tall is not one stone and never was: it is eight drums of 0.90 (D) each, hoisted
# one on another, and the joint between two of them is the thing that tells a quarried column from
# an extruded one at any distance where the flutes have gone. There are six shafts here at 2.70 m
# centres in the deepest shadow in the building, and this is what the eye has left to read them by
# when the 0.030 fillets have dissolved into the ambient.
#
# IT IS MATTER AND NOT A WEATHERING COAT, and that distinction is the whole reason this may be here
# at all while the file's header still says NO WEATHERING. A weathering coat displaces the WHOLE
# surface by a fraction of a voxel and sands the one-voxel fillets off everywhere at once. A bed
# joint is a groove 0.045 (M/10) tall — the same number as the gap between two dentils, 1.44 voxels
# at the contract's metre 32, so it genuinely relocates a surface rather than dithering one — cut at
# seven heights and nowhere else. Between two of them 0.855 m of fillet and flute survives
# untouched, which is twenty-seven voxels of the thin matter this part exists to measure, seven
# times over per shaft.
#
# THE CUTTER FOLLOWS THE ENTASIS, and it has to: the shaft swells 0.050 over its taper at a third of
# its height, so a groove cut by a cylinder of one radius misses the stone at the top and buries
# itself at the middle — which is the trap `_order.clip` names for the flutes themselves. So the
# cutter is the shaft's own arc struck 0.0675 (3M/20) smaller, revolved: parallel to the surface all
# the way up, and shrunk in the PROFILE for the same reason the flute floor above is, or the groove
# would be a bore down the axis instead of a ring round the outside.
#
# 0.0675 is the flute depth 0.0375 plus 0.030 (M/15, the fillet's own width). So the groove stands
# 0.030 below the FLOOR OF A FLUTE and 0.0675 below a fillet, and it is therefore a continuous ring
# rather than twenty-four dashes between the fillets — which is what a cutter shallower than the
# flutes would have left, and is not a bed joint.
#
# It can reach nothing but my own shaft: the cutter is the shaft's own revolve intersected with
# seven bands inside its height, so it is a subset of the shaft by construction, and the nearest
# matter of anybody's to a portico shaft is 1.35 m away across the intercolumniation.
#
# The seed sits at y = 0 of its own repeat and the finished line is carried to 3.60 afterwards — the
# rule `_order.clip` gives for `repeat`, and the reason is that a copy straddling a cell boundary
# makes the whole expression infinitely slack. Seven copies at 0.90 land on 0.90 .. 6.30 of the
# shaft's own 0 .. 7.20, which is eight drums of D.
let portico_drum_floor = revolve {
    intersection { shaft_band  offset { shaft_arc } by=0.0675 }
} axis=y
let portico_drum_skin  = difference { shaft_solid portico_drum_floor }
let portico_drum_seed  = box -0.60 -0.0225 -0.60   0.60 0.0225 0.60
let portico_drum_bands = translate { repeat { portico_drum_seed } y=0.90 ny=3 } 0 3.60 0
let portico_drum_cut   = translate {
    intersection { portico_drum_skin portico_drum_bands }
} 0 0.45 0

let portico_col_unit = difference {
    union { ionic_column portico_col_shoe portico_bed }
    portico_flutes portico_drum_cut
}

let portico_col_in  = translate { portico_col_unit } 1.35 1.80 -11.15
let portico_col_mid = translate { portico_col_unit } 4.05 1.80 -11.15
let portico_col_out = translate { portico_col_unit } 6.75 1.80 -11.15

let portico_columns = mirror { union { portico_col_in portico_col_mid portico_col_out } } axis=x

# ================================================================================================
# THE ANTAE — the order returned to the wall at z = -7.50
# ================================================================================================
#
# One at each end, on the axis of the corner column (x = +-6.75), standing against the south face
# of the main block. `ionic_pilaster` is drawn with its wall face on z = 0 projecting to z = +0.18,
# so a half turn about y points it south, which is the way this building faces.
#
# The anta is what the flank of the portico lands on: without it the coffered ceiling arrives at
# the wall carried by nothing, and the eye reads a 5 m soffit floating off a flat wall.
#
# THE ANTAE LIVE HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE. pilasters.clip stood two of its own for a while, at
# x = 7.20, half a module out from these; two Ionic capitals 0.45 apart at the end of the
# elevation is the worst thing that could happen to this front, and it agreed to drop them. Its
# nearest minor pilaster now starts at x = 9.45. If you are adding pilasters to the south wall,
# start there and leave x = 6.75 alone.
let portico_anta_shoe = box -0.675 -0.05 -0.05   0.675 0.05 0.18   round=0.015

let portico_anta_unit = union { ionic_pilaster portico_anta_shoe portico_bed }
let portico_anta_turn = rotate { portico_anta_unit } y=0.5
let portico_anta_east = translate { portico_anta_turn } 6.75 1.80 -7.50

let portico_antae = mirror { portico_anta_east } axis=x

# ================================================================================================
# THE SOFFIT — the coffered ceiling, its underside on y = 9.90
# ================================================================================================
#
# A slab 0.90 (D) thick from 9.90 to 10.80, over the whole portico from x -7.65 to 7.65 (the
# contract's portico_x, 17M) and from the column axis at z = -11.15 back to z = -7.35 — which is
# 0.15 (M/3) INSIDE the wall face at -7.50, so the ceiling and the wall certainly meet however far
# the manifest's grain moves the wall's surface.
#
# It stops at the column axis and not in front of it. Everything south of -11.15 at this height
# belongs to the entablature, whose architrave sits on these abaci and whose corona hangs out over
# them; any face I put out there would either be buried or would read as a ledge under theirs. The
# north half of each abacus is 0.36 deep and 1.08 wide, which is all the bearing this needs.
let portico_soffit_slab = box -7.65 9.90 -11.15   7.65 10.80 -7.35  round=0.02

# THE COFFERS. `coffer_unit` is a void 0.90 (D) square, so the grid is laid out on a pitch of
# 1.35 (3M) and the band of stone between two coffers is 0.45 (M) — the classical 2:1.
#
# IN X the bands land on the column axes, which is where a ceiling's beams belong: the coffer
# centres are the half-pitches between them, +-0.675, +-2.025, +-3.375, +-4.725, +-6.075, so a
# band 0.45 wide sits centred on 0, +-1.35, +-2.70, +-4.05, +-5.40 and +-6.75. Ten across.
#
# IN Z, from the column axis northwards: a plain band 0.90 (D) wide over the columns, which is the
# soffit of the architrave and is not coffered; then a row, a 0.45 band, and a second row. That
# accounts for 3.15 of the 3.65 between the column axis and the wall, and THE REMAINING 0.50 IS
# THE ONE NUMBER IN THIS FILE THAT IS NOT A MODULE FRACTION. It cannot be: the contract makes the
# portico 5.00 m deep and 5.00 is not a whole number of 0.45. It is taken at the wall, because
# that is where a real ceiling has its widest member and where the wall's own cornice will land.
#
#   -11.15 .. -10.25   architrave band     0.90  (D)
#   -10.25 ..  -9.35   coffer row, centre -9.80
#    -9.35 ..  -8.90   band                0.45  (M)
#    -8.90 ..  -8.00   coffer row, centre -8.45
#    -8.00 ..  -7.50   the remainder, against the wall
#
# The void is dropped 0.02 below the ceiling plane so the opening is cut past the rounding on the
# first step's bottom arris and reads as a clean 0.90 square.
#
# A repeat is safe here where a polar repeat would not be: the copy is 0.90 across inside a 1.35
# period, so it fits in its own cell, and the field returns a true distance and can still settle
# boxes. The seed sits at x = 0 for exactly that reason — a shape straddling a cell boundary makes
# the whole thing infinitely slack — and the finished line is carried out to 3.375 afterwards, so
# the five copies land on 0.675, 2.025, 3.375, 4.725 and 6.075.
let portico_cof_seed  = translate { coffer_unit } 0 9.88 -9.80
let portico_cof_line  = repeat { portico_cof_seed } x=1.35 nx=2
let portico_cof_row_s = translate { portico_cof_line } 3.375 0 0
let portico_cof_row_n = translate { portico_cof_row_s } 0 0 1.35
let portico_coffers   = mirror { union { portico_cof_row_s portico_cof_row_n } } axis=x

let portico_soffit = difference { portico_soffit_slab portico_coffers }

# THE ROSETTES, named again so they can be gilded. `coffer_unit` leaves a 0.18 (0.4 M) square boss
# standing in the middle of each pan, hanging 0.075 (M/6) below it. This is the same block in the
# same twenty places — it paints the boss, it adds no matter of its own, and it is the reason the
# hardest thing in this scene is at the back of a hollow rather than out where the sun is.
let portico_ros_seed  = box -0.09 10.030 -9.89   0.09 10.105 -9.71  round=0.018
let portico_ros_line  = repeat { portico_ros_seed } x=1.35 nx=2
let portico_ros_row_s = translate { portico_ros_line } 3.375 0 0
let portico_ros_row_n = translate { portico_ros_row_s } 0 0 1.35
let portico_rosettes  = mirror { union { portico_ros_row_s portico_ros_row_n } } axis=x

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART
# ================================================================================================

let part_portico = union { portico_soffit portico_columns portico_antae }

# This part removes nothing from anybody. Both cuts in this file — the coffers out of my own slab,
# the flutes and drum joints out of my own shafts — are taken inside the expression that builds the
# thing they are cut from, so neither can reach another fragment's stone by construction. The
# manifest never has to know they exist and there is no `void_portico`.
#
# THAT IS NOT THE SAME AS SAYING THEY SURVIVE, and for a year the coffers did not. `difference` is
# local; `union` is not. entablature.clip's portico block occupied the same volume as this slab
# from 9.90 to 11.90 and was unioned over it in the manifest, so all twenty coffers were refilled
# with its stone — measured at 13216 voxels, which is exactly what the cut below takes out. A cut
# that cannot reach anybody is still only half the question; the other half is whether anybody
# reaches back over it, and no single file can answer that about itself. See requests/portico.md.
# let void_portico = <nothing>

# --- paint ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Ceiling first, columns over it, gilt last, because a later coat covers an earlier one and the
# rosettes are inside the ceiling. `below=0.02` on every rule, never 0: the manifest displaces the
# whole building by 12 mm at the end and a test at zero misses the voxels that displacement made.
paint limestone where=portico_soffit   below=0.02
paint marble    where=portico_columns  below=0.02
paint marble    where=portico_antae    below=0.02
paint gilt      where=portico_rosettes below=0.02
